{
  "an10.22:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.22 ",
  "an10.22:0.2": "3. The Great Chapter ",
  "an10.22:0.3": "Hypotheses ",
  "an10.22:1.1": "Then Venerable Ānanda went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: ",
  "an10.22:2.1": "“Ānanda, I claim to be self-assured regarding the teachings that lead to realizing by insight the various different hypotheses. ",
  "an10.22:2.2": "So I am able to teach the Dhamma in appropriate ways to different people. Practicing accordingly, when something exists they’ll know it exists. When it doesn’t exist they’ll know it doesn’t exist. When something is inferior they’ll know it’s inferior. When it’s superior they’ll know it’s superior. When something is not supreme they’ll know it’s not supreme. When it is supreme they’ll know it’s supreme. ",
  "an10.22:2.3": "And they will know or see or realize it in whatever way it should be known or seen or realized. This is possible. ",
  "an10.22:2.4": "But this is the unsurpassable knowledge, that is: truly knowing each and every case. ",
  "an10.22:2.5": "And Ānanda, I say that there is no other knowledge better or finer than this. ",
  "an10.22:3.1": "The Realized One possesses ten powers of a Realized One. With these he claims the bull’s place, roars his lion’s roar in the assemblies, and turns the divine wheel. ",
  "an10.22:3.2": "What ten? ",
  "an10.22:3.3": "Firstly, the Realized One truly understands the possible as possible, and the impossible as impossible. 